# Shell scripts and container inputs must keep LF even when checked out on Windows, or they will not run on
# Linux (container / CI / server). Under core.autocrlf=true, a CRLF gradlew makes the container build die with
# `bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M`, and a CRLF Dockerfile breaks the line continuations (\) in RUN.
gradlew         text eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.sql           text eol=lf

# ★ These are REQUIRED, not nice-to-have. Checked out as CRLF on a Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true:
#     .env.example  → values copied into .env gain a trailing \r. OAUTH_ISSUER_URI becomes
#                     "http://host:{{PORT}}\r", so the string the Resource Server verifies differs character
#                     for character from the `iss` the Authorization Server stamps — and the app starts
#                     perfectly while every request 401s.
#     .dockerignore → a pattern becomes ".env\r" and can fail to match. Those patterns are the only thing
#                     keeping credentials out of the build context.
.env.example    text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf

# Windows batch keeps CRLF
*.bat           text eol=crlf
gradlew.bat     text eol=crlf

# ⚠ THIS FILE FIXES LINE ENDINGS ONLY — it cannot fix the executable bit.
#   git on Windows records newly added files as 100644, so gradlew gets committed non-executable and a CI
#   checkout fails with `./gradlew: Permission denied`. On the first commit you MUST run:
#       git update-index --chmod=+x gradlew setup.sh
#   Verify:  git ls-files -s gradlew   → must read 100755
#   (The workflow and the Dockerfile also chmod +x as a second line of defence.)
